<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hama is a city on the Orontes river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, famous for its great wooden water wheels, the norias. As Hamath it was the capital of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdom until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> annexed it in 720 BCE, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was ruled by a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a>, including the historian-prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abu al-Fida</a>. In 1982 the Syrian government crushed an uprising there, killing thousands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hama.jpg" alt="Hama" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hama,_Panoramic_view,_Syria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
